<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Victoria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nyanza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Victoria is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, shared by Uganda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Tanzania, and the main source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>. Kingdoms grew on its shores, including Buganda in the north, the Busoga principalities and the Haya kingdoms such as Karagwe and Kianja in the west. European explorers reached it from 1858, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> made Buganda the core of the Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-victoria.jpg" alt="Lake Victoria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS042-E-263206_-_View_of_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Victoria is one of the African Great Lakes. It covers about 59,947 square kilometres and is the world's largest tropical lake and the second-largest fresh water lake by surface area after Lake Superior. It lies in a shallow depression, with an average depth of 40 metres and a maximum of about 80 metres, and its catchment area covers 169,858 square kilometres. Tanzania holds 49 per cent of the lake, Uganda 45 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> 6 per cent.</p><p>The lake receives 80 per cent of its water from direct rainfall, the rest from rivers and streams, the largest of which is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a> on the western shore. Its only outflow is the Nile River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, Uganda. One account puts the lake's age at about 400,000 years: it formed as westward-flowing rivers were backed up when a block of the Earth's crust tilted along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a>. Its shores are home to haplochromine cichlids, of which more than 500 species are almost all found nowhere else.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded information about the lake comes from Arab traders who travelled the inland routes in search of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, other goods and slaves. Many African peoples lived in the catchment area. The explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a>, travelling with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a>, was the first European to see it, reaching its southern shore in 1858. He named it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and believed he had found the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><p>Burton, who was recovering from illness at Kazeh, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>, rejected the claim as unproven. A very public quarrel followed, which provoked debate among scientists and drew other explorers to confirm or refute Speke's view. In 1952 engineers acting for the government of colonial Uganda blasted out the natural rock weir at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, which had controlled the outflow for at least 12,000 years, and replaced it with a barrage. Uganda later added the Kiira Hydroelectric Power Station, completed in 2002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake supports <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s largest inland fishery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> perch and several tilapias were introduced in the 1950s, and the Nile perch in particular is blamed for the extinction of many native cichlids; the loss has been called the most dramatic example of human-caused extinctions within an ecosystem. Kisumu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entebbe">Entebbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, Mwanza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a> are among the towns and cities on or near the shore. Ferries have been an important means of transport between Uganda, Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> since the 1900s, and the main ports are Kisumu, Mwanza, Bukoba, Entebbe, Port Bell and Jinja.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Victoria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gold was found near <b>Lake Victoria</b> in 1894, and the Sekenke Gold Mine opened in 1909.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Buganda began as a small kingdom on the northern and north-western shores of <b>Lake Victoria</b>, around the hills where Kampala stands today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the eighteenth century the balance turned: Buganda, with more centralised command and war canoes on <b>Lake Victoria</b>, took Buddu and other border districts, and the secession of Toro around 1830 deprived Bunyoro of rich pastures and the salt lakes of the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Uganda is a fertile, well-watered country around <b>Lake Victoria</b> and the upper Nile, and most Ugandans have always lived by farming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about the 16th century the land between the Nile, <b>Lake Victoria</b> and Lake Kyoga was divided among numerous small principalities, many of whose ruling families traced their origins to Bunyoro and its legendary hunter Mukama.</blockquote><a href="/state/busoga-principalities">Busoga principalities</a></li><li><blockquote>The mukama (king) ruled with chiefs and clan heads over a dense farming population growing bananas, coffee and beans on the fertile shores of <b>Lake Victoria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ihangiro">Kingdom of Ihangiro</a></li></ul></section>
